    "q-351550": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351550",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2x-3}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,\\,21\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $24$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $8$ | $21$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2x-3}{x-3}={x}^{2}+2x+8+\\frac{21}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2x-3}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x+8+\\frac{21}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x+21{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+8x\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+21\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})+({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})+8(1-0)+21(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0-3|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+1+8+21(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{3}+21\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
